C. Instrument

The eOpen instrument is intended to be of value to academic, scientific, and social communities interested in open education, educational innovation, research evaluation, and complex environments. The instrument designed in [68] measures students’ perceptions of knowledge, skills, and attitudes or values related to open education and its subcompetencies from a 30-item questionnaire in the following.

1) Capacity building (items 1–8);
2) Development of support policies (items 9–15);
3) Promotion of effective, inclusive, and equitable access (items 16–21);
4) Creation of sustainability models (items 22–25);
5) Promotion of international cooperation (items 26–30);

The eOpen instrument uses a four-point Likert scale (1: Strongly disagree, 2: Disagree, 3: Agree, and 4: Strongly agree) to measure the students’ level of self-assessment.

